{"id": 13, "ts": 1780160376.8806498, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 1, "agent": "agent_2", "message": "I appreciate Agent\u202f1\u2019s detailed endorsement of Candidate\u202fA, especially the points on anticipation of danger, complex\u2011systems insight and leadership. Those strengths indeed map well to operational reliability, technical competence and crew cooperation. However, the same feedback also flagged that Candidate\u202fA is \u201cnot open to new ideas\u201d and is \u201cregarded as a show\u2011off,\u201d which raises concerns about professional communication and adaptability\u2014both critical for long\u2011distance operations where feedback loops and crew harmony are essential.\nGiven the new evidence from Agent\u202f3 that Candidate\u202fD handles unexpected events adequately (suggesting decent stress resilience) and that Candidate\u202fB sometimes uses the wrong tone and is seen as pretentious (indicating communication lapses), we have a clearer picture of the communication and stress dimensions across the pool. Candidate\u202fB\u2019s strong conscientiousness and stress handling are offset by those communication issues, while Candidate\u202fD\u2019s responsibility and problem\u2011solving are tempered by a hot temper and a know\u2011it\u2011all attitude.\nBalancing these, I remain inclined to recommend **Candidate\u202fA** because the core safety\u2011critical competencies (anticipation, complex\u2011connection insight, spatial vision, leadership) outweigh the relatively moderate concerns about openness and demeanor, which can be mitigated through targeted onboarding and mentorship. The other candidates each have a more pronounced weakness (B\u2019s communication tone, C\u2019s egocentricity and lack of further education, D\u2019s hot temper) that directly affect crew cooperation and professionalism.\nThus, my current vote stays with **Candidate\u202fA**."}
